blocks.explanation:
When white light falls on a compound:
• if it is absorbed completely, the complex appears black;
• if the whole light is reflected or transmitted, it appears white;
• if some light is absorbed and the rest reflected, the complex shows a colour that depends on the light reflected.

d-d transition: in a complex the d orbitals are split. An electron in a lower-energy d orbital absorbs light and is promoted (excited) to a higher-energy d orbital. The energy needed corresponds to a specific frequency and wavelength of visible light by Planck's equation, ΔE = hν = hc/λ.

Colour seen: the unabsorbed light is transmitted and decides the colour. The colour we see is the complementary (opposite) colour of the light absorbed, as on the colour wheel (Figure 18.6). For example, a complex that absorbs yellow light appears violet.
